# gnome-cisco-vpn

GNOME Shell extension (UUID `cisco-vpn@charisma.ir`) for Cisco AnyConnect via `openconnect` + TOTP. Ubuntu 24.04–26.04, Shell 45–50.

## Deploy before testing

Changes in repo are NOT live until copied:

```bash
rsync -av --exclude='.git' . ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions/cisco-vpn@charisma.ir/
# Alt+F2 → r
```

## Key files

- `extension.js` — panel UI, 2s status monitor
- `vpnManager.js` — connect/disconnect orchestration
- `network.js` — PID + `cscovpn0` tunnel/IP only
- `notifier.js` — use `Main.notify(title, body)` only
- `bootstrap.js` — DI container

## Architecture rules

- VPN interface is fixed: `cscovpn0` (`constants.js` → `Paths.INTERFACE_NAME`)
- `network.connected()` = own openconnect PID exists AND `cscovpn0` up
- `getVpnIp()` reads only `cscovpn0`, never generic `tun*`
- Show IP in UI only when `state.isConnected()`
- No NetworkManager/nmcli — shell commands only

## Known pitfalls

- Do NOT rewrite `notifier.js` with MessageTray — broke all notifications
- GNOME 46 queues rapid `Main.notify` banners; use delayed `connected()` + cancel on disconnect
- Late `connect()` completion must be guarded with `_connectAttempt`
- `extension2.js` / `extension3.js` are legacy — edit `extension.js` only
- Only commit when user explicitly asks

See `GUIDE.md` for full Persian dev guide.
